  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-({x}^{2}-6)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+2x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-4x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\  & =(2-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-2x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =(2-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-2x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+2x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ over the interval $[0,\\pi ].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/410e0af2ed20a065c27da5edaf0095dfd22c8a37a92e3d8fbaeece629cefa278.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x<0$ when $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<\\pi .$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{\u2033}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x>0,$ which has the solution $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
